# Instructions:
# Please make sure that you follow the rules correctly to avoid accidentally
# committing unwanted files or excluding files that should be tracked.
# This is expecially important for instances where 
# 
# Quick reference:
#   *        matches anything except '/' — stays within one directory level
#   **       matches across directory levels (zero or more directories)
#   ?        matches exactly one character (except '/')
#   [abc]    matches any ONE character in the set: a, b, or c
#   [a-z]    matches any ONE character in the range a through z
#   !        negates/re-includes a pattern
#   /prefix  anchors to this directory
#   suffix/  matches directories only
#
# Order matters: rules are evaluated top to bottom, last match wins.
# A negation (!) must come AFTER the pattern it's meant to override.
#
# WARNING: if a DIRECTORY is ignored (e.g. build/), Git never looks
# inside it — no negation below can un-ignore files within it, no
# matter where it's placed. To keep specific files, exclude the
# directory's CONTENTS instead:
#   build/*
#   !build/keep.txt
# ...rather than ignoring the directory itself:
#   build/          <- negations below this can't reach inside
#
# CASE SENSITIVITY: patterns are case-sensitive on Linux (default
# filesystems), but effectively case-INsensitive on macOS and Windows
# (case-insensitive filesystems by default). A pattern like Build/
# won't match build/ on Linux but will on Mac/Windows — always match
# the exact case of the real file/folder name to avoid cross-platform
# surprises (e.g. a rule that works locally but fails on Linux CI).
#
# Examples:
#   debug?.log    matches debug1.log, debugA.log — not debug10.log
#   [Dd]ebug/     matches Debug/ or debug/
#
# Full syntax: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitignore

# Ignore anything not meant to be in the article
/data
/src
/docs